# Corporate Technical Documentation Style Guide

## Tone, Voice, and Technical Perspective
*   **Tone:** Professional, objective, direct, and user-centric.
*   **Voice:** Use **active voice** instead of passive voices wherever possible.
    *   **Correct**: "The system updates the database table index."
    *   **Incorrect**: "The database table index is updated by the system."
*   **Perspective**: Address users using the **second-person perspective** ("you"). Do not use first-person pronouns like "I", "we", or "our".

## Headings and Structural Organization
*   Every document must contain exactly **one Tier-1 Heading (`#`)** acting as the main title.
*   Use sequential heading patterns (`##`, `###`, `####`). Never skip a heading tier (e.g., do not place an `####` immediately below an `##`).
*   Write all headings in either sentence case or camel case depending on the preferred organizational style.
    *   **Sentence case**: Capitalize only the first word and proper nouns.
    *   **Camel Case**: Capitalize each word in a sentence. 

## UI Element Representation Standard
When directing a user through a software interface, format the UI interactions using these exact visual conventions:
*   **Buttons, Tabs, and Fields:** Wrap the literal UI labels inside bold text markers (e.g., Click the **Submit Transaction** button).
*   **Menu Navigation Chains:** Separate sequential navigation items using bold markers and right-pointing angle indicators (e.g., Select **File** > **Preferences** > **Keymaps**).
